---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ImagePlaceholder from '../../components/ImagePlaceholder.astro';
import StatChip from '../../components/StatChip.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const meta = [
  { label: 'Role', value: 'UX designer — internal tools' },
  { label: 'Context', value: 'Ubisoft' },
  { label: 'Timeline', value: '2021 — 2024' },
  { label: 'Scale', value: '1,000+ users' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Enterprise Internal Tools"
  eyebrow="Case study — Enterprise tools"
  heading="Enterprise Internal Tools — Rebuilding trust in complex planning systems"
  subtitle="Planning, staffing, and governance tools used by 1,000+ people had drifted into inconsistency. I led the audit and redesign that made approvals, quotas, and performance data legible again."
  heroLabel="Enterprise — planning & governance console"
  meta={meta}
  seoDescription="Enterprise internal tools case study — rebuilding trust in planning, staffing, and governance workflows used by 1,000+ people."
>
  <section class="reveal">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Context</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <p>
        At <strong>Ubisoft</strong>, planning and staffing decisions ran through internal tools used by
        more than <strong>1,000 people</strong> across production. Over years of incremental additions,
        the surfaces had fragmented — dozens of one-off modals, inconsistent approval flows, and quota
        rules that were hard to see and easy to misread.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal mt-8">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-4">
      <StatChip value="1,000+" label="Users across teams" />
      <StatChip value="40+" label="Modals audited" />
      <StatChip value="1" label="Coherent system" />
      <StatChip value="3" label="Workflow domains" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Problem</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <p>
        The tools worked, but people didn&rsquo;t trust them. Approvals felt opaque, quota visibility
        was buried, and performance-aware data was scattered across surfaces that behaved differently.
        When a system that governs staffing feels unpredictable, people route around it — and
        governance quietly breaks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">My role</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <p>
        <strong>UX designer for internal tools</strong>, leading the audit and redesign in partnership
        with product, engineering, and operations. I owned the research, the systemization, and the
        governance/workflow improvements — from messy inventory to a coherent interaction model.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Research</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <ul>
        <li>Inventoried and mapped <strong>40+ modals</strong> to expose duplication and inconsistency.</li>
        <li>Traced real approval and quota workflows to find where trust broke down.</li>
        <li>Interviewed cross-functional users to separate policy problems from interface problems.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8">
      <ImagePlaceholder label="Enterprise — 40+ modal audit map" ratio="video" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Design systems & process</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <ul>
        <li>Consolidated one-off modals into consistent, reusable patterns.</li>
        <li>Made quota visibility a first-class part of the interface, not a buried number.</li>
        <li>Designed approval flows with clear status, ownership, and next steps.</li>
        <li>Built performance-aware data interfaces that stayed readable under real density.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8">
      <Callout kind="quote">
        In enterprise tools, trust is a feature. If people can&rsquo;t see why a decision happened,
        they stop believing the system.
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Governance & workflow improvements</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <p>
        By aligning the interface with how governance actually needed to work, approvals became
        traceable, quotas became visible at the point of decision, and the same rules read the same way
        everywhere. Cross-functional alignment turned a pile of modals into a system people could
        reason about.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <ImagePlaceholder label="Enterprise — approval flow" ratio="video" />
      <ImagePlaceholder label="Enterprise — quota visibility" ratio="video" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Impact</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <ul>
        <li>A fragmented tool set became one coherent, trustworthy system.</li>
        <li>Approval and quota workflows became legible to the people who depend on them.</li>
        <li>Consistency reduced confusion and the workarounds that erode governance.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">What this demonstrates</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <ul>
        <li>Taming complex, high-stakes enterprise UX at real scale.</li>
        <li>Systemizing chaos — turning 40+ modals into one coherent model.</li>
        <li>Designing for governance, approvals, and performance-aware data.</li>
        <li>Driving cross-functional alignment across product, engineering, and operations.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
